  "difficulty": 1,
  "prompt": "A Ferris wheel car starts at the right side of the wheel and rotates $400^\\circ$. What is the smallest positive angle that would have left it in exactly the same spot? Give just the number of degrees.",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "40",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "One full trip around the wheel is $360^\\circ$ — that much rotation puts you right back where you started.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "One full trip around the wheel is $360^\\circ$ — that much rotation puts you right back where you started."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "Peel off one full turn: compute $400^\\circ - 360^\\circ$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$400^\\circ - 360^\\circ = 40^\\circ$, so the car ends up $40^\\circ$ around the wheel."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "A full rotation is $360^\\circ$, and rotating a full turn changes nothing about where you end up.",
    "So subtract one full turn from $400^\\circ$: $400^\\circ - 360^\\circ = 40^\\circ$.",
    "Angles that land in the same place are called coterminal, so $400^\\circ$ and $40^\\circ$ are coterminal."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Think of it as an odometer",
      "steps": [
        "Rotation is like a trip counter that resets every $360^\\circ$.",
        "Going $400^\\circ$ is one complete lap plus $40^\\circ$ of a second lap.",
        "The lap does not show, so all you see is the extra $40^\\circ$."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tags": [
    "coterminal",
    "standard-position"
  ],
